1. Optimized Linux File System Layout and Storage Management
Scenario Analysis
The server has 50 GB total storage and must store rapidly growing system logs, user files, and application data.
The major risk is that logs may consume the entire disk and prevent critical services from operating.
Total = 50 GB

The important design decision is to keep /var separate from /. 
Since system logs are normally stored under /var/log, a separate /var prevents rapidly growing logs from filling the root filesystem.
Log Rotation
Use logrotate to control log growth.
Example:
sudo nano /etc/logrotate.d/systemlogs
Configuration:
/var/log/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 10
    size 1G
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
}

This ensures old logs are rotated, compressed and eventually removed.

Check configuration:

sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf

Force rotation:

sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf
Monitoring Disk Usage
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*

Set up monitoring so that an administrator is alerted when /var approaches its capacity.

Ensuring Logs Do Not Exceed 10 GB

The /var partition is limited to 10 GB, and logrotate continuously rotates and compresses old logs. 
Disk monitoring can trigger cleanup/alerts before the partition becomes completely full.

Result

The partitioned design isolates logs, user data and applications. Therefore, excessive log growth 
cannot easily consume the space required by the operating system, helping critical services continue operating.
2. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads
Scenario Analysis

The system experiences frequent context switching delays during high CPU workloads. 
Hardware upgrades are unavailable, so optimization must be performed at the kernel level.
1. Scheduler Tuning

Linux scheduler parameters can be examined and tuned using:

sysctl -a | grep sched

For suitable workloads, CPU scheduling behaviour can be adjusted to reduce unnecessary scheduling overhead.

2. CPU Affinity

Bind a CPU-intensive process to selected CPUs:

taskset -cp 2,3 <PID>

This reduces unnecessary movement of the process between CPUs and can improve cache locality.

3. Process Priority

Use:

nice -n -5 ./program

or modify an existing process:

sudo renice -5 -p <PID>

Critical workloads can receive higher scheduling priority where appropriate.

4. Real-Time Scheduling

For real-time workloads, Linux provides real-time scheduling policies such as:

chrt -f 80 ./program

This should be used carefully because excessive real-time priority can starve normal processes.

5. Kernel Modules

Kernel modules allow functionality to be added or removed without rebuilding the entire kernel.

Useful commands:

lsmod
modinfo <module>
sudo modprobe <module>
sudo modprobe -r <module>

Only required modules should be loaded to reduce unnecessary kernel overhead.

Result

Combining scheduler tuning, CPU affinity, appropriate priorities and carefully selected kernel modules can reduce scheduling 
overhead and improve responsiveness for CPU-intensive and real-time workloads without requiring hardware upgrades.
3. Processing 1 Million Log Entries Using Shell Scripting
Scenario Analysis

The administrator needs to process 1 million log entries per day and the script must finish within 2 minutes.

Therefore, the solution should minimize:

External command calls
Repeated file reading
Shell loops
Unnecessary disk I/O
Inefficient Approach

Avoid:

while read line
do
    grep "$line" logfile
done

This repeatedly launches commands and is inefficient for large files.

Optimized Approach

Use a single-pass command such as:

awk '{count[$5]++} END {for (x in count) print x, count[x]}' logfile

Here, awk reads the file once and processes records in memory.

Example

Suppose the log contains:

10:01 INFO Login
10:02 ERROR Database
10:03 INFO Login
10:04 WARNING Memory

Command:

awk '{count[$2]++} END {for (x in count) print x,count[x]}' logfile
Useful Commands

grep – fast pattern searching:

grep "ERROR" logfile

awk – field processing and aggregation:

awk '$3=="ERROR" {count++} END {print count}' logfile

sort – sorting:

sort logfile

uniq – counting repeated entries:

sort logfile | uniq -c

For very large files, combine commands carefully to avoid unnecessary processing.

Optimized Script
#!/bin/bash

LOGFILE="server.log"

awk '
$3 == "ERROR" { errors++ }
$3 == "WARNING" { warnings++ }
$3 == "INFO" { info++ }

END {
    print "INFO:", info
    print "WARNING:", warnings
    print "ERROR:", errors
}
' "$LOGFILE"
Performance Justification

awk is suitable because it:

Processes the file in a single pass
Avoids spawning a separate command for every line
Performs filtering and counting in one operation
Reduces disk I/O
Uses efficient internal processing

For 1 million entries, this approach is much more suitable than repeatedly invoking 
commands inside a shell loop and is designed to meet a tight execution-time requirement.

4. Managing a Faulty Process Consuming 90% CPU
Scenario Analysis

A background process is consuming 90% CPU. The system cannot be restarted, and critical services must remain unaffected.

Therefore, the process should first be identified and monitored, then controlled safely.

Process Management Strategy
High CPU Usage
      ↓
Identify Faulty Process
      ↓
Monitor CPU Usage
      ↓
Check Process Details
      ↓
Reduce Priority
      ↓
Monitor Again
      ↓
Still Consuming CPU?
    ↙             ↘
  NO               YES
  ↓                 ↓
Continue       Terminate Safely
Monitoring          ↓
                Verify Services
1. Identify Process

Use:

top

or:

ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head

This displays processes consuming the highest CPU.

2. Monitor the Process
top -p <PID>

or:

pidstat -p <PID> 1
3. Inspect Process
ps -fp <PID>

Check which service/application owns the process before taking action.

4. Reduce Priority

If the process is not critical:

sudo renice 10 -p <PID>

This gives other processes a higher scheduling preference.

5. Stop the Process Gracefully

First attempt:

kill <PID>

Then verify:

ps -p <PID>

Only if it fails to terminate should a stronger signal be considered:

kill -9 <PID>
6. Protect Critical Services

Before terminating the process:

systemctl status <critical-service>

After intervention:

systemctl status <critical-service>
Result

The faulty process can be identified, monitored, deprioritized and, if necessary, terminated without rebooting the server. This preserves the operation of critical services.

5. Resource Allocation for 5 Virtual Machines
Given
Physical RAM = 16 GB
Number of VMs = 5
Minimum RAM per VM = 2 GB
Host OS requirement = 4 GB
Calculate Required RAM

VM requirement:

5 × 2 GB = 10 GB

Host requirement:

4 GB

Total:

10 GB + 4 GB = 14 GB

Available:

16 GB

Remaining:

16 - 14 = 2 GB

Therefore, the requirements are feasible.

Proposed Allocation
Component	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM1	2 GB
VM2	2 GB
VM3	2 GB
VM4	2 GB
VM5	2 GB
Reserve	2 GB
Total	16 GB

Memory Management

The remaining 2 GB should be kept as a safety reserve rather than immediately assigning it to VMs.

If supported by the virtualization platform, techniques such as:

Memory ballooning
Dynamic memory allocation
Memory overcommitment

can be used carefully.

However, because each VM has a minimum requirement of 2 GB, the minimum allocation should not be reduced below that level.

Result

All five VMs can operate with their required minimum memory while the host retains its required 4 GB. 
Keeping 2 GB as reserve provides additional
protection against temporary memory pressure and improves overall system stability.
